module gunbc.scm.repository_save

// WRITING A REPOSITORY TO A PATH, WITH THE ADJUDICATION AHEAD OF THE WRITE.
//
// The mirror of `gunbc.scm.repository_load`, split from it for the reason `extdeps.languages.json`
// splits `parse` from `emit`: reading and writing are two directions of one grammar, not one
// operation with a mode flag. They fail differently and are consumed separately -- a caller that
// only loads must not link a writer.
//
// THIS MODULE EXISTS BECAUSE `encode_repository` CANNOT REFUSE. It returns a JsonValue, so its only
// honest treatment of an unrepresentable repository is an empty reference that `decode_repository`
// rejects later. `repository_envelope`'s header states the consequence, the whole reason this
// module calls the CHECKED encoder: by the time an unrepresentable repository is caught on load,
// the bytes are on disk, the write has reported success, and the corruption surfaces to WHOEVER
// LOADS IT rather than whoever caused it. Lifting the adjudication ahead of the write keeps the
// fault with its author.
//
// SO THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT INCIDENTAL: encode-checked, THEN write. No arm in this type
// reports a write for a repository that failed adjudication, because no such call is made -- the
// refusal returns before `Filesystem.Write` is reached. That is the construction form of the rule
// (DESIGN section 5), not a check that the write was skipped.
//
// THE THREE ARMS HAVE TWO DIFFERENT OWNERS, the same discipline the load side carries:
//
//   save refused by codec  the CALLER's fact about the VALUE. This repository cannot be written by
//                          any writer to any path: a commit root absent from the store, a checkout
//                          selecting a commit that is not there. Changing the path does not help.
//   file unwritable        the HOST's fact about the PATH. The repository is well-formed; the
//                          directory is missing, or read-only, or the disk is full. Changing the
//                          path may well help.
//
// Collapsing them would tell a caller to fix their repository when their directory is read-only,
// or to check permissions when their repository is inconsistent. Opposite remedies, as on the load
// side.
//
// EVERY ARM CARRIES THE PATH, as the load arms do: a refusal that does not say what it was writing
// is not located.
//
// NOT `WriteOwnerOnly`. A repository document is not a secret, and narrowing its mode here would be
// a deployment policy fact smuggled into the persistence operation -- the layer inversion DESIGN
// section 3 names. A caller with a confidentiality requirement states it; this module does not
// decide it on their behalf.

import std.types { String, Int }
import std.content_hash {
  content_hash_eq_structural,
  Fnv1a64Structural,
}
import extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io
import extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io { FilesystemEstablishedAbsence, filesystem_established_absence_path, filesystem_listing_names_entry }
import extdeps.languages.json.emit { serialize_json, JsonValue }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size }
import std.checked_arithmetic { nat_magnitude }
import gunbc.scm.object_shard_json { object_shard_json_text, object_shard_name_for, object_shard_path_for, shard_partition, shard_members_of, shard_identity_of, envelope_parent_dir, envelope_file_name }
import gunbc.scm.object_store { ScmObject, scm_object_identity }
import gunbc.scm.repository_envelope {
  RepositoryEnvelope,
  RepositoryEncodeOutcome,
  RepositoryEncoded,
  RepositoryEncodeRefused,
  RepositoryEncodeRefusal,
  encode_repository_checked,
  encode_repository_sharded_checked,
  repository_generation_of_content,
}

// THE BYTE COUNT IS A MAGNITUDE, NOT A NUMBER, and it is carried as one. `ByteSize` is
// `std.measure`'s `Measure<Memory, One, Nat>`, the single authority for a quantity of bytes, so
// this row reuses the magnitude the repository already models rather than a bare `Int` whose unit
// lives only in a field name. The raw scalar is legitimate at the `extdeps.filesystem` boundary
// under cited-spec fidelity -- the host really does report an `Int` -- and what the rule forbids
// is propagating it inward, the step this type used to take (review 56828).
//
// AND THE CROSSING REFUSES RATHER THAN CLAMPS, which is why there is a fourth arm. `ByteSize`
// counts a `Nat`, the host reports an `Int`, and the only available crossing
// (`std.checked_arithmetic` `nat_magnitude`) takes an ABSOLUTE VALUE -- applied to a negative it
// would turn a nonsense report into a plausible byte count, the fabricated-plausible-output
// failure in one line. So the negative is refused as its own arm carrying what the host said, and
// `nat_magnitude` is applied only on the non-negative domain where it is the identity. A boundary
// obligation (DESIGN section 4b): external reality observed, typed, and admitted or refused -- not
// a class on the ladder, so no next-rung trigger.
//
// NO WITNESS COVERS THAT FOURTH ARM AND THAT IS A LIMIT, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Reaching it requires a
// host write that SUCCEEDS and then reports a negative count, which no substrate-only fixture can
// author -- this module's witness is `SubstrateInputsOnly`, and a real write refuses at the
// hermetic boundary before any count is produced. So the arm is reachable by the mechanism and
// unreachable by any input this repository can currently express. It is kept because the
// alternative is not "no arm" but `nat_magnitude` silently turning the negative into a byte
// count; deleting the arm would delete the refusal, not the case.
//
// THE GUARDED ENTRY GROWS THE OUTCOME RATHER THAN THIS TYPE, for the reason init.dag's fold and
// render.dag's renderer state: RepositorySave's existing consumers answer questions an
// unguarded write can ask (init's create, the renderer's lines), and arming them to match
// guard-only refusals they can never produce would be the callee-arm-carried-past-its-exclusion
// shape this stack keeps removing. The compare-and-set refusals live on
// RepositoryGuardedSave beside an arm embedding RepositorySave whole, so one save vocabulary
// serves both entries and no consumer matches an arm its route cannot construct.
type RepositorySave
  = RepositorySaved { path: String, written: ByteSize }
  | RepositorySaveRefusedByCodec { path: String, cause: RepositoryEncodeRefusal }
  | RepositoryFileUnwritable { path: String, error: String }
  | RepositoryWriteByteCountUnrepresentable { path: String, reported: Int }

// THE GENERATION A GUARDED SAVE EXPECTS ON DISK, as one sealed question. GenerationExpected
// carries the hash the writer's load observed; GenerationExpectedAbsent records that the load
// observed NO envelope -- the first-save case. The distinction is carried as data rather than a
// bare optional because the two expectations refuse differently when the disk disagrees: an
// observed generation that no longer matches is STALE, while an observed absence that is no longer
// absent means an envelope APPEARED, and "someone else saved after you loaded" and "someone
// created what you saw as empty" are different facts about different remedies, not one boolean's
// two values.
type RepositoryGenerationExpectation
  = GenerationExpected(Fnv1a64Structural)
  | GenerationExpectedAbsent

// THE PURE HALF OF THE COMPARE-AND-SET GUARD, separated from the effects so a witness reaches
// every arm at the hermetic boundary. The judgment is total over (expectation, observed
// generation): an absent on-disk envelope ADMITS, because a save over absence destroys nothing --
// the first-save path stays admissible, and an envelope that vanished between load and save
// leaves nothing a stale writer could clobber. A present on-disk generation that differs from
// what the writer observed -- in either direction, older or newer -- is STALE, and the refusal
// carries BOTH identities, because a stale refusal that names only one side sends its operator to
// compare against a generation they cannot see.
type RepositoryGenerationAdmission
  = RepositoryGenerationAdmitted
  | RepositoryGenerationStale { expected: RepositoryGenerationExpectation, found: Fnv1a64Structural }

fn admit_repository_generation(
  expected: RepositoryGenerationExpectation,
  found: Fnv1a64Structural?,
) -> RepositoryGenerationAdmission {
  match found {
    Absent => RepositoryGenerationAdmitted
    Present { value: on_disk } =>
      match expected {
        GenerationExpectedAbsent =>
          RepositoryGenerationStale { expected: expected, found: on_disk }
        GenerationExpected(observed) =>
          if content_hash_eq_structural(left: observed, right: on_disk) {
            RepositoryGenerationAdmitted
          } else {
            RepositoryGenerationStale { expected: expected, found: on_disk }
          }
      }
  }
}

// WHAT THE GUARD OBSERVED AT THE ENVELOPE'S ADDRESS, one instant before the write. Absence is
// established by a successful listing never naming the file -- the same rule the belt's envelope
// observation carries -- and a listed-then-unreadable envelope is UNOBSERVABLE, not absent: a
// compare-and-set decided on a guess would be worse than none, so the guard fails closed and the
// save refuses.
type EnvelopeGenerationObservation
  = EnvelopeGenerationObserved { found: Fnv1a64Structural? }
  | EnvelopeGenerationUnobservable { error: String }

fn observe_envelope_generation(path: String) -> EnvelopeGenerationObservation {
  let listing = Filesystem.List(path: envelope_parent_dir(path: path))
  if !listing.success {
    EnvelopeGenerationUnobservable {
      error: join(["the envelope's directory could not be listed, so the on-disk generation is unknown: ", listing.error], ""),
    }
  } else if !filesystem_listing_names_entry(listing: listing.entries, name: envelope_file_name(path: path)) {
    EnvelopeGenerationObserved { found: none }
  } else {
    let read = Filesystem.Read(path: path)
    if read.success {
      EnvelopeGenerationObserved { found: Present { value: repository_generation_of_content(content: read.content) } }
    } else {
      EnvelopeGenerationUnobservable {
        error: join(["the envelope is listed but could not be read, so the on-disk generation is unknown: ", read.error], ""),
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE OUTCOME OF A COMPARE-AND-SET GUARDED SAVE. The embedded arm carries the unguarded save
// outcome whole -- codec refusal, unwritable path, nonsense byte count, and success are the same
// facts under a guard as without one -- and the two guard-only arms name the ways a compare-and-set
// can refuse: the on-disk generation moved since the writer loaded (STALE, carrying both
// identities), or the on-disk generation could not be observed at all (so the set half of the
// compare-and-set cannot run, and the save fails closed rather than writing unguarded).
type RepositoryGuardedSave
  = RepositoryGuardedSaveOutcome(RepositorySave)
  | RepositoryStaleGenerationRefused { path: String, expected: RepositoryGenerationExpectation, found: Fnv1a64Structural }
  | RepositoryGuardedSaveUnobservable { path: String, error: String }

// CREATING A REPOSITORY IS NOT SAVING ONE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WRITE ITSELF.
//
// save_repository writes unconditionally, which is correct for its subject: persisting a repository
// that already exists must replace the bytes that are there. Initialization is the opposite subject
// -- it must happen ONLY where nothing exists -- and for a long time it was expressed as "observe an
// absence, then call save_repository". That is two operations with a gap, and an actor creating the
// file in the gap makes the save truncate exactly the bytes the observation was taken to protect.
// The observation was honest; the write was not conditioned on it.
//
// No re-observation closes that. The fix is that the existence test and the creation become ONE act,
// which is what Filesystem.WriteCreateNew provides -- so this function refuses a path that exists
// rather than checking whether it exists. Construction over validation (DESIGN section 5) at a
// boundary where validation structurally cannot win.
//
// The refusal reuses RepositoryFileUnwritable rather than minting a create-specific arm: the caller's
// remedy is the same -- the bytes are not yours to write and the host says why -- and the error text
// is the host's own. A second arm would be a second spelling of one outcome (section 3), and one that
// classified the cause from the error TEXT would be a heuristic standing in for an observation.
// THE CREATE THAT AN ESTABLISHED ABSENCE AUTHORIZES, AND THE ONLY ONE IT CAN.
//
// create_repository below takes a bare String, which is right for its own subject -- a caller that
// has some other reason to create at a path may use it. It is the WRONG entry for a caller whose
// permission came from an absence, because taking the token and a separate path lets the two name
// different subjects and nothing detects it. Review on gunbc#10026 found exactly that: init matched
// ScmInitMayCreate(_), threw the token away, and wrote to an independently computed path.
//
// So the absence-authorized create derives its target from the token itself. There is no second
// target to disagree with, which is the difference between a capability that is CONSUMED and one
// that is merely carried past the actuation.
fn create_repository_at_absence(
  absence: FilesystemEstablishedAbsence,
  repo: RepositoryEnvelope,
) -> RepositorySave {
  create_repository(path: filesystem_established_absence_path(absence: absence), repo: repo)
}

fn create_repository(path: String, repo: RepositoryEnvelope) -> RepositorySave {
  match encode_repository_checked(repo: repo) {
    RepositoryEncodeRefused { cause: cause } =>
      RepositorySaveRefusedByCodec { path: path, cause: cause }
    RepositoryEncoded { value: v } => {
      let write = Filesystem.WriteCreateNew(path: path, content: serialize_json(v: v))
      if !write.success {
        RepositoryFileUnwritable { path: path, error: write.error }
      } else if write.bytes_written < 0 {
        RepositoryWriteByteCountUnrepresentable { path: path, reported: write.bytes_written }
      } else {
        RepositorySaved { path: path, written: byte_size(count: nat_magnitude(a: write.bytes_written)) }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn save_repository(path: String, repo: RepositoryEnvelope) -> RepositorySave {
  match encode_repository_checked(repo: repo) {
    RepositoryEncodeRefused { cause: cause } =>
      RepositorySaveRefusedByCodec { path: path, cause: cause }
    RepositoryEncoded { value: v } => {
      let write = Filesystem.Write(path: path, content: serialize_json(v: v))
      if !write.success {
        RepositoryFileUnwritable { path: path, error: write.error }
      } else if write.bytes_written < 0 {
        RepositoryWriteByteCountUnrepresentable { path: path, reported: write.bytes_written }
      } else {
        RepositorySaved { path: path, written: byte_size(count: nat_magnitude(a: write.bytes_written)) }
      }
    }
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SHARDED WRITE (v7). The index document is the commit point: shards go down first (write-once
// -- a shard's name is its content identity, so an existing shard with that name already holds
// these bytes, and it is skipped rather than rewritten), and the index is written LAST, so an
// interrupted save leaves the previous index naming only shards that exist. A save after one
// candidate's change rewrites that candidate's few new shards and the small index, never another
// object's bytes -- the delta write the monolith could not express.
//
// THE SHARDS ARE FLAT SIBLINGS OF THE INDEX, because the Filesystem interface has no mkdir and the
// directories a repository lives in already exist: an envelope at <dir>/<name>.gunbc-repository.json
// shards into <dir>/<name>.object.<digest>.json.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------






type ShardWriteAcc {
  written: Int
  refused: String?
}

// ONE ORDERING AUTHORITY FEEDS BOTH THE FILES AND THE ROSTER: the shard writer partitions the
// store's OWN order -- the exact list the index encoder rosters -- so the files written and the
// names named can never disagree (the first grain rostered a dependency re-sort while writing a
// raw partition, and named fourteen files with only twelve in common; the fix is not to re-sort
// harder but to stop re-sorting). The stored order loads back exactly as written and store
// mutations append, so a save after one candidate's change rewrites the open tail shard and the
// index, never a sealed shard's bytes.
fn save_object_shards(envelope_path: String, objects: List<ScmObject>) -> ShardWriteAcc {
  let parent_dir = envelope_parent_dir(path: envelope_path)
  let listing = Filesystem.List(path: parent_dir)
  if !listing.success {
    ShardWriteAcc {
      written: 0,
      refused: Present {
        value: join(["the envelope's directory could not be listed, so which shards exist is unknown: ", parent_dir, " (", listing.error, ")"], ""),
      },
    }
  } else {
    let present = fold(
      split(s: listing.entries, delimiter: "\n") |> filter(e => trim(e) != ""),
      init: empty_map(),
      f: fn(acc, name) { map_insert(acc, trim(name), true) },
    )
    fold(
      shard_partition(objects: objects),
      init: ShardWriteAcc { written: 0, refused: none },
      f: fn(acc, shard) {
        match acc.refused {
          Present { value: _ } => acc
          Absent => {
            let digest = shard_identity_of(member_digests: shard_members_of(objects: shard))
            let shard_name = object_shard_name_for(envelope_path: envelope_path, identity_digest: digest)
            match map_lookup(m: present, key: shard_name) {
              Present { value: _ } => acc
              Absent => {
                let shard_path = object_shard_path_for(envelope_path: envelope_path, identity_digest: digest)
                let write = Filesystem.Write(
                  path: shard_path,
                  content: object_shard_json_text(objects: shard),
                )
                if write.success {
                  ShardWriteAcc { written: acc.written + 1, refused: none }
                } else {
                  ShardWriteAcc {
                    written: acc.written,
                    refused: Present { value: join(["the object shard could not be written: ", shard_path, " (", write.error, ")"], "") },
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      },
    )
  }
}

// THE SHARDED WRITE'S SHARED TAIL: shards first (write-once, content-named, skipped when present),
// the index last, so an interrupted save leaves the previous index naming only shards that exist.
// Both the unguarded entry and the guarded entry reach this only through the checked encoder --
// the adjudication stays ahead of every write either way.
fn persist_sharded_repository(path: String, repo: RepositoryEnvelope, v: JsonValue) -> RepositorySave {
  match save_object_shards(envelope_path: path, objects: repo.store.objects) {
    ShardWriteAcc { written: _, refused } =>
      match refused {
        Present { value: detail } =>
          RepositoryFileUnwritable { path: path, error: detail }
        Absent => {
          let write = Filesystem.Write(path: path, content: serialize_json(v: v))
          if !write.success {
            RepositoryFileUnwritable { path: path, error: write.error }
          } else if write.bytes_written < 0 {
            RepositoryWriteByteCountUnrepresentable { path: path, reported: write.bytes_written }
          } else {
            RepositorySaved { path: path, written: byte_size(count: nat_magnitude(a: write.bytes_written)) }
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn save_repository_sharded(path: String, repo: RepositoryEnvelope) -> RepositorySave {
  match encode_repository_sharded_checked(repo: repo) {
    RepositoryEncodeRefused { cause: cause } =>
      RepositorySaveRefusedByCodec { path: path, cause: cause }
    RepositoryEncoded { value: v } =>
      persist_sharded_repository(path: path, repo: repo, v: v)
  }
}

// THE COMPARE-AND-SET GUARDED SAVE. The adjudication order is the module's own law, one level
// deeper: encode-checked FIRST (a repository that no writer can write refuses before the
// filesystem is consulted), THEN the generation observation, THEN the write -- so no arm in this
// type reports a write for a repository that failed adjudication, and no stale arm performs one.
// The caller's expectation is the generation its load observed (RepositoryLoaded carries it beside
// the envelope for exactly this), or GenerationExpectedAbsent when the load observed no envelope;
// the guard refuses when the disk no longer matches that observation, which is the whole content
// of the protection. The 2026-09-22 incident was a stale writer -- a save derived from a
// commit-0-only load, executed four minutes after a capture had persisted ordinal 1 -- silently
// rewinding the envelope, and no arm existed to say the world had moved.
//
// THE REFUSAL IS THE GUARD'S ONLY EFFECT ON A STALE WORLD: the stale arm returns before
// persist_sharded_repository is reachable, so a refused guarded save leaves every byte on disk --
// shards and index -- exactly as it found them, and the next tick's fresh load re-derives the
// write against the generation that is actually there.
fn save_repository_sharded_guarded(
  path: String,
  repo: RepositoryEnvelope,
  expected: RepositoryGenerationExpectation,
) -> RepositoryGuardedSave {
  match encode_repository_sharded_checked(repo: repo) {
    RepositoryEncodeRefused { cause: cause } =>
      RepositoryGuardedSaveOutcome(RepositorySaveRefusedByCodec { path: path, cause: cause })
    RepositoryEncoded { value: v } =>
      match observe_envelope_generation(path: path) {
        EnvelopeGenerationUnobservable { error } =>
          RepositoryGuardedSaveUnobservable { path: path, error: error }
        EnvelopeGenerationObserved { found } =>
          match admit_repository_generation(expected: expected, found: found) {
            RepositoryGenerationAdmitted =>
              RepositoryGuardedSaveOutcome(persist_sharded_repository(path: path, repo: repo, v: v))
            RepositoryGenerationStale { expected: stale_expected, found: stale_found } =>
              RepositoryStaleGenerationRefused { path: path, expected: stale_expected, found: stale_found }
          }
      }
  }
}
